== n1  2026-11-17  A/B: c3's plan, kit, reference and voices with --no-assets (every shot draws every character itself)  |  1 director round
result: 46.7 s, 53 calls, 824k tokens, $0.445 (c3's matching phases, assets + shots + review: 527k tokens, $0.246); output tokens 301k vs 146k; shot code 10-22k chars vs 3-10k; slow shots 150-200 s per request vs 15-60 s. Wall time not comparable (my bug: the old "largest asset under 380 px" rule rejected every no-asset shot on the first pass; fixed: skipped when a reel has no assets)
look: Mara is a different person in almost every shot (glasses and purple marks like a moustache in the hook, purple hair in shot 2, long hair in shot 3, half-hidden blocky face in shot 7); cafe shot characters left as grey outlines; a paper held over a face again; hook punch-in fills the frame with one face
better: freer acting where it worked (rubbing an eye, reaching for the alarm, yawning, smiling in bed at the end)
verdict: shared drawings win on consistency, reliability and cost (about half the tokens); their weakness is acting limited to the planned poses. Next idea (user's): plan the distinct pose variations up front, draw them from one character model, check them as a pose sheet, shots pick from the menu; group drawings for recurring compositions (character in bed)

